WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.130
<v Instructor>Okay, so let's get into EMR.</v>

2
00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.770
EMR stands for Elastic MapReduce,

3
00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.200
and they help you create Hadoop clusters

4
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.710
to do Big Data in the AWS Cloud.

5
00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.540
The idea is that you would use EMR

6
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.390
if you're doing a migration from on-premise,

7
00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:19.050
and many companies have an on-premise Hadoop cluster,

8
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.720
and they wanna migrate these jobs onto AWS

9
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.220
to take advantage of the Cloud.

10
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.110
So usually it's about elasticity.

11
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.860
So, the clusters can be made of hundreds of EC2 instances,

12
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.620
so they give you a lot of scale to process data.

13
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:32.910
But then as soon as you're done,

14
00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.370
you're more than welcome to shut down your EMR cluster

15
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.500
and that saves you a lot of cost as well.

16
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.300
So EMR is a natural fit

17
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.230
to migrate your Hadoop clusters from on-premise onto AWS.

18
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.950
EMR is going to be launched in the Big Data ecosystem.

19
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.140
So it's going to be made of Apache Spark,

20
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.550
HBase, Presto, Flink, Hive, et cetera, et cetera.

21
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.810
The idea is that usually

22
00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.370
Hadoop clusters are quite complicated

23
00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.930
to create and configure,

24
00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.900
but with EMR, all the provisioning and the configuration

25
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.120
of the EC2 instances is done for you.

26
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.060
You just specify what you want and you get it very quickly.

27
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.700
You also get auto-scaling capability with CloudWatch,

28
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.220
and the use cases for EMR very classically

29
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.410
is data processing, machine learning,

30
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.240
web indexing, big data,

31
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.300
basically anything you need to process at scale.

32
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.330
Now, EMR has a few integrations you need to remember

33
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.800
going into the exam.

34
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.530
So EMR is launched within a VPC,

35
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.830
and it is within a single availability zone.

36
00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.750
So you have your EC2 instances

37
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.280
and they have EBS volumes

38
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.810
and you can have many of that.

39
00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.720
And the EBS volumes are running something called HDFS

40
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.850
for Hadoop Distributed File System,

41
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.890
and that gives you temporary storage

42
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.800
for your EC2 instances in your cluster.

43
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.220
But if you want to have long-term retention of data,

44
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.590
you need to use something called EMRFS,

45
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.750
which has a native integration into S3,

46
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.460
and S3 obviously is going to be

47
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.490
a more durable type of storage because it is multi-AZ,

48
00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.560
and that gives you permanent storage

49
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.290
and you can also define server-side encryption.

50
00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.140
So as we can see, any EBS volume is going to be

51
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.910
high performance for temporary storage

52
00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.410
and for your computations,

53
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.060
but as soon as you need to persist your data

54
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.850
into something that's going to be multi-AZ,

55
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.730
use EMRFS and S3.

56
00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.400
As you can see, everything is launched in your EMR cluster

57
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.890
within a single-AZ because you get natural,

58
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.890
better networking performance and lower cost.

59
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.020
But obviously because it is within a single-AZ,

60
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.370
in case you lose your AZ

61
00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.080
you would lose your data.

62
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.590
Then we can run something like Apache Hive on EMR,

63
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.050
and there is a module that has been developed for Hive

64
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.870
to directly read data from a DynamoDB table.

65
00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.480
So in case you want to analyze all your DynamoDB table,

66
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.990
you can do it so from EMR by using Hive.

67
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.020
Okay, next, very important, you need to understand

68
00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.960
how you can optimize your cost in EMR.

69
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.530
So Amazon EMR is made of clusters of EC2 instances

70
00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.000
and you have different kind of nodes.

71
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.110
You have the master node,

72
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.300
and the master node manages the cluster.

73
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.280
It will coordinate and manage the health

74
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.110
of all your other nodes

75
00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.540
and it must be long running.

76
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.950
Then you have the core node.

77
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:08.610
They're here to run tasks and also store data,

78
00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.620
and they must be long running as well.

79
00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.390
And then you have this task node,

80
00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.340
which is there just to run tasks

81
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.010
and usually you can take spot instances for it,

82
00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.860
and using task node is optional.

83
00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.440
So the purchasing options really depends.

84
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.230
If you have the on-demand EC2 instance type,

85
00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.640
then you're going to get reliable,

86
00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.440
predictable type of workloads

87
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.480
and they will never be terminated.

88
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.160
If you use reserved instances,

89
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.770
and remember it's minimum one year of usage,

90
00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.380
then you're going to get big cost savings

91
00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.270
and EMR will automatically use

92
00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.490
your reserved instances if possible.

93
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:45.030
And therefore, good candidates for reserved instances

94
00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.980
are going to be the master node

95
00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.840
because it must be long running,

96
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.360
and also the core nodes.

97
00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.790
And regarding spot instances,

98
00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.500
well because they can be terminated,

99
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.680
they're less reliable but they're cheaper.

100
00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.400
And so, a good way for you to leverage spot instances

101
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.353
would be on Task Nodes.

102
00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.260
Finally, for EMR in terms of deployments,

103
00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.390
you can have either long-running clusters,

104
00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.900
which would make sense for than using reserved instances,

105
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.330
or you can use transient temporary clusters

106
00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.740
to just do something

107
00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.560
and then tear it down once the analysis is done.

108
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.410
Okay, next you have the instance configuration.

109
00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.450
So you have uniform instance groups,

110
00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.430
where you select a single instance type

111
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.230
and purchasing option for each node.

112
00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.677
So in this example, you say,

113
00:04:29.677 --> 00:04:32.430
"Okay, the master needs to be on-demand or spot,

114
00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.550
the cores, I want two and to be on-demand or spot,

115
00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:39.570
and the task node, I want c4 xlarge, D22 xlarge,"

116
00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:40.403
that kind of things.

117
00:04:40.403 --> 00:04:41.910
And you say on-demand spot.

118
00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.110
So this is very helpful,

119
00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:46.020
and it's called a uniform instance group.

120
00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.500
A difference, the other type of EMR instance configuration

121
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.390
is called an instance fleet,

122
00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.520
where you select a target capacity

123
00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.060
and you will mix instant types and purchasing options.

124
00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.627
So in this example, you're saying,

125
00:04:58.627 --> 00:05:02.040
"Okay, my master is going to be an on-demand.

126
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.400
My core can be a bunch of different instances,

127
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.200
and six of them needs to be on-demand

128
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.150
and 10 of them needs to be spot.

129
00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:10.470
And same for my tasks."

130
00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:11.643
So you have more,

131
00:05:12.661 --> 00:05:15.210
it's a spot fleet basically for EMR.

132
00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.520
So that's how the best way to describe it.

133
00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.290
So it's just two purchasing options.

134
00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.120
The first one has auto-scaling,

135
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.820
the second one does not have auto-scaling for now,

136
00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.100
but the idea is that on the left-hand side

137
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.410
you choose the instance type in advance.

138
00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:29.640
And on the right-hand side,

139
00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.400
you just specify a mix of on-demand and spot fleet

140
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.310
and a bunch of different instance types you can choose from.

141
00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.770
And EMR will provision that cluster for you.

142
00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.990
I don't think the exam will go into that level of details,

143
00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.540
but it's good for you to know as a solution architect

144
00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.760
that these two options are available.

145
00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.860
Okay, so that's all you need to know for EMR.

146
00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:47.820
I hope you like this lecture

147
00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.770
and I will see you in the next lecture.

